One D & D characteristic that hasn't made it into nethack yet is age.
[Of course objects like food can age and rot but not monsters/players]
Depending on character class (elves live longer), one would have a lifetime
assigned, probably fuzzed out some by a random number of years.  The
time in the dungeon would be used to increment your age.  You could then
be killed by "old age".  Monsters could get an age attack as could wands
and spells.  Possibly, the value could be tuned to correspond to likely
intervals of winning.  One could be accorded experience points for each
age increment.  Fountains could turn into fountains of youth and reduce
one's age.  Being touched by an undead monster might age one.  One could
have a potion of youth.  A cursed potion ages one, of course.

A secondary use of age could be in fleshing out the #chat code.  Monsters
could reply to you dependent on your age such as "a young ogresnapper" or
"an old fog cloud".  This could be merged with the present grow_up()
logic.

Major medical problems could be associated with extreme age before one
actually croaks.  Say after 90% of ones lifetime one needs a cane to
walk; after 95%, one needs a wheelchair?

The seduction code could use age as well.  Extemely old and young characters
could be ignored.

I'm sure we are all looking forward to a future version of nethack with
color 3d holographic output with smells.

